A 2016 Cochrane Review of 35 randomised controlled trials found exercise had a 'large and significant' effect on depression compared to control conditions.
Exercise is as effective as antidepressants for mild-to-moderate depression in multiple head-to-head trials, including the landmark SMILE trial at Duke University (Blumenthal et al., 1999, 2007).
Regular exercise reduces the risk of developing depression by 26%, according to a meta-analysis of 49 studies covering 266,939 people published in JAMA Psychiatry (2019).
Exercise increases hippocampal volume — the brain region that shrinks in depression — by up to 2% over 12 weeks, directly reversing one of depression's neurological signatures.
